## Step 1: Decide the track for each claim

Every claim you extract MUST be classified into exactly one of two tracks:

- **"objective"** — general or technical knowledge NOT specific to this applicant's
  own project. This is checkable against public documentation or reference sources.
  Examples: "PostgreSQL supports JSON columns", "FastAPI is built on Starlette",
  "Prolog is a low-level language" (this example is actually FALSE — Prolog is a
  high-level/declarative language — but that doesn't matter here: your job right now
  is only to classify the track, not to judge correctness).

- **"project_specific"** — a claim about the applicant's OWN project: their results,
  their dataset, their architecture choices, their measured performance. No public
  source can confirm or deny these.
  Examples: "Our CNN model achieved 96% accuracy", "The dataset contains 50,000 images".

If you are unsure which track a claim belongs to, default to "project_specific" —
it is safer to under-claim verifiability than to send an unverifiable personal claim
down the fact-checking path.

## Step 2: For each claim, output

- text: the claim, verbatim from the slide (do not paraphrase or translate it)
- claim_type: one of performance | dataset | architecture | technology | algorithm | capability | business
- track: "objective" or "project_specific" (per Step 1 above)
- importance: high | medium | low — how much this claim matters to evaluating the
  applicant's technical competence (a core performance metric is high; a stylistic
  or cosmetic detail is low)
- subject, property, value, unit: ONLY if the claim states a measurable quantity
  (e.g. subject="model", property="accuracy", value=95, unit="%"). Leave these null
  if the claim is qualitative (e.g. "We used a modular architecture").

## What is NOT a claim

Skip: greetings, agenda/section titles, "Thank you" / "Questions?" slides, pure
opinion ("I really enjoyed this project"), and vague statements with no checkable
content ("This project was challenging").

Return a JSON array of claim objects. Return an empty array `[]` if the slide has
no checkable claims at all.